For “authentic or not,” you first have to distinguish between Chengdu hot pot, Chongqing hot pot, and other styles. You can’t just look at whether it’s spicy enough. Focus on whether the soup base is fragrant rather than only oily and spicy, whether the layers of beef tallow and spices are natural, whether ingredients like beef tripe, yellow throat, and duck intestines are fresh, crisp, and tender, and whether the menu and dipping sauces match local habits; then combine that with reviews from local diners, table turnover rate, and back-kitchen hygiene to judge. Clothes being marinated with the flavor doesn’t equal authenticity either—it’s more an issue of oil smoke and ventilation 😂
You can remove the smell like this:
1. When you get back to where you’re staying, hang your outerwear in a ventilated place first. Don’t stuff it into the suitcase right away;
2. Go over it once with steam from a garment steamer, or hang it outside the bathroom while showering to borrow some moisture, then dry it thoroughly afterward;
3. Wash washable clothes according to the care label, adding a small amount of baking soda when soaking; you can also add a small amount of white vinegar separately during the rinse stage, but don’t mix the two;
4. Don’t randomly soak delicate fabrics like wool, down, or leather. Prioritize airing them out or sending them for professional cleaning;
5. Wash your hair thoroughly twice first, especially the roots and the back of your head, then blow it dry; if it’s temporarily inconvenient to wash it, use dry shampoo spray as an emergency measure.
When we book the restaurant, let’s also check the reviews on smoke exhaust while we’re at it. That can stop us from being “walking hot pot people” for fewer days right from the source.

